"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the sub\u2011problem. For \\(n=3\\) there exists a configuration of three distinct lines that covers all required lattice points and contains exactly one sunny line.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. List the required points for \\(n=3\\):  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]  \n2. Choose two non\u2011sunny lines (parallel to the axes) that together cover five of the six points:  \n   * \\(L_{1}: y=1\\) (horizontal).  \n   * \\(L_{2}: x=1\\) (vertical).  \n3. The only uncovered point is \\((2,2)\\).  \n4. Add a sunny line through \\((2,2)\\) that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); for instance \\(L_{3}: y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n5. Verify point\u2011by\u2011point that every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and that exactly one line (\\(L_{3}\\)) is sunny.\n\nThus \\(k=1\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\u201d yields the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must exhibit three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly one of the lines is *sunny* (i.e. its slope is neither \\(0\\), nor \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\)).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\; y=1,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{2}&:\\; x=1,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{3}&:\\; y=x .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n* \\(L_{2}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n* \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\); it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)). Therefore \\(L_{3}\\) is sunny.\n\nNow we check each point of \\(S\\):\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line(s)? | Verification |\n|------------------|------------------------|--------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713; \\(x=1\\) \u2713; \\(y=x\\) gives \\(1=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(y=x\\) gives \\(2=2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n\nAll six points are covered, the three lines are distinct, and exactly one line (\\(L_{3}\\)) is sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) sunny line exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=1\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]"
