0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\nHence exactly one of the three lines is *non‑sunny*; denote it by \\(L\\).\nWe consider all possibilities for the direction of \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  \\(L\\) vertical  (\\(x=c\\))\n\nThe only possible columns intersecting \\(S\\) are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n*If \\(c=1\\):*  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nAny line through two of these three points has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{2-3}=0,\\qquad\n\\frac{2-1}{2-2}=\\infty,\\qquad\n\\frac{1-2}{3-2}=-1,\n\\]\n\nall prohibited for a sunny line. Consequently each sunny line can contain\nat most one of the three points, so two sunny lines can cover at most two\nof them, leaving one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=2\\):*  \\(L\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \nPairs among these points have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) except the pair\n\\((1,2),(3,1)\\) whose slope is \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (sunny). Thus at most one sunny\nline can cover two of the remaining points; the other two points would each\nrequire a separate sunny line, exceeding the allowed two sunny lines.\nContradiction.\n\n*If \\(c=3\\):*  symmetric to the case \\(c=1\\).  \n\nHence a vertical non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  \\(L\\) horizontal  (\\(y=c\\))\n\nBy symmetry with the vertical case the same reasoning shows that no\nhorizontal non‑sunny line can coexist with two sunny lines covering all\npoints of \\(S\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  \\(L\\) of slope \\(-1\\)  (\\(x+y=c\\))\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le4\\) for points of \\(S\\), the only possible values are\n\\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n*Subcase \\(c=4\\):*  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nAny line through two of these three