d slope). Hence two sunny lines cannot cover all four points \u2013\n  contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  Symmetric to the case \\(c=1\\); the same contradiction arises.\n\nConsequently a vertical non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(L\\) horizontal: \\(L:\\;y=c\\).\n\nBy symmetry with the vertical case (swap the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)),\nthe same reasoning shows that no horizontal non\u2011sunny line can coexist\nwith two sunny lines covering all points of \\(S\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(L\\) of slope \\(-1\\): \\(L:\\;x+y=c\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(S\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le4\\), the only possible values\nare \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(c=4\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Their pairwise slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), all prohibited. Hence at most\n  two of them can be covered by the two sunny lines, leaving one uncovered \u2013\n  contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Computing all slopes:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\ (\\text{allowed})\\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n  (2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The only admissible sunny line among these points is the line through\n  \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); it covers exactly those two points. The points\n  \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) remain uncovered and any line joining them has\n  slope \\(-1\\), which is prohibited. Hence the four points cannot be\n  covered by two sunny lines \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Their admissible sunny pairs are\n\n  \\[\n  (1,2)-(3,1):\\ \\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\qquad\n  (1,3)-(2,1):\\ \\text{slope }-2 .\n  \\]\n\n  These two lines cover the four points\n  \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\) respectively, leaving the\n  point \\((2,2)\\) uncovered.  The point \\((2,2)\\) cannot be placed on a sunny\n  line together with any other uncovered point because all such slopes are\n  \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence two sunny lines cannot cover all five\n  points \u2013 contradiction.\n\nSince every possible choice for the unique non\u2011sunny line leads to a\ncontradiction, a configuration of three distinct lines covering all points\nof \\(S\\) with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion:**  \nFor \\(n=3\\) there is no arrangement of three distinct lines that satisfies\nthe covering condition and has exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.  Hence the\nrequested configuration is impossible."
