false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le 4\\}\n   =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must find three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly two of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. their slopes are different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\nHence precisely one of the three lines is *non‑sunny*; denote it by \\(L\\).\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\nvertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), or slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\nWe examine each possibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(L\\) vertical: \\(L:\\;x=c\\).\n\nThe only columns intersecting \\(S\\) are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Their pairwise slopes are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(2,1)-(2,2):\\ \\infty,\\qquad\n  (2,1)-(3,1):\\ 0,\\qquad\n  (2,2)-(3,1):\\ -1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  All three are prohibited for a sunny line; therefore any sunny line can\n  contain at most one of these three points. Two sunny lines can cover at\n  most two of them, leaving one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n  Computing slopes between every pair we obtain\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n  (1,2)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\ (\\text{allowed})\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\ (\\text{prohibited})\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  Thus the only admissible sunny line among these points is the line\n  through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\). It covers exactly those two points; the\n  remaining points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) cannot be placed on any sunny\n  line (any line through either of them and another uncovered point has a\n  prohibited slope). Hence two sunny lines cannot cover all four points –\n  contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  Symmetric to the case \\(c=1\\); the same contradiction arises.\n\nConsequently a vertical non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(L\\) horizontal: \\(L:\\;y=c\\).\n\nBy symmetry with the vertical case (swap the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)),\nthe same reasoning shows that no horizontal non‑sunny line can coexist\nwith two sunny lines covering all points of \\(S\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(L\\) of slope \\(-1\\): \\(L:\\;x+y=c\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(S\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le4\\), the only possible values\nare \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(c=4\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Their pairwise slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), all prohibited. Hence at most\n  two of them can be covered by the two sunny lines, leaving one uncovered –\n  contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Computing all slopes:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\ (\\text{allowed})\\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n  (2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The only admissible sunny line among these points is the line through\n  \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); it covers exactly those two points. The points\n  \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) remain uncovered and any line joining them has\n  slope \\(-1\\), which is prohibited. Hence the four points cannot be\n  covered by two sunny lines – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Their admissible sunny pairs are\n\n  \\[\n  (1,2)-(3,1):\\ \\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\qquad\n  (1,3)-(2,1):\\ \\text{slope }-2 .\n  \\]\n\n  These two lines cover the four points\n  \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\) respectively, leaving the\n  point \\((2,2)\\) uncovered.  The point \\((2,2)\\) cannot be placed on a sunny\n  line together with any other uncovered point because all such slopes are\n  \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence two sunny lines cannot cover all five\n  points – contradiction.\n\nSince every possible choice for the unique non‑sunny line leads to a\ncontradiction, a configuration of three distinct lines covering all points\nof \\(S\\) with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion:**  \nFor \\(n=3\\) there is no arrangement of three distinct lines that satisfies\nthe covering condition and has exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.  Hence the\nrequested configuration is impossible.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the reasoning is sound, but lacks an explicit justification step. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**Location:** \"p≡1