false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof for all claimed values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist configurations with exactly  \n  – \\(k=0\\) sunny lines (all lines horizontal), and  \n  – \\(k=1\\) sunny line (one sunny line together with \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines).  \n\n* No configuration can exist with a number of sunny lines \\(k\\) satisfying  \n  \\[\n  2\\le k\\le n-2 .\n  \\]\n\nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remain unproved; I was unable to construct a covering of the triangular lattice with exactly \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines, nor to show that such coverings are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   A line is *non‑sunny* if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (lower bound).**  \n   In any family of sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\) at least \\(n\\) sunny lines are needed.  \n   *Proof.* The vertical line \\(x=1\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots,(1,n)\\).  \n   A sunny line is never vertical, so it can contain at most one of those points. ∎  \n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\); none of them is sunny.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x + (n-1).\n   \\]  \n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b=n\\) is \\((1,n)\\) and lies on \\(L\\); all other points lie on one of the horizontal lines. Exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n5. **Impossibility for intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists and \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Delete all non‑sunny lines; the remaining uncovered points form a translate of the set  \n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]  \n   (Each deleted non‑sunny line removes an entire horizontal, vertical, or diagonal “layer’’ of points; after removing \\(n-k\\) such layers the shape that remains is precisely a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).)  \n\n   By Lemma 1, covering \\(T_k\\) with sunny lines needs at least \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   *If \\(k=2\\):* the three points of \\(T_2\\) cannot be covered by only two sunny lines (a direct check shows that any two non‑vertical, non‑horizontal, non‑\\(-1\\)‑slope lines can contain at most two of the three points). Hence a configuration with \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n\n   *If \\(k\\ge 3\\):* covering \\(T_k\\) already requires exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Lemma 1 gives the lower bound \\(k\\), and the \\(k\\) points on the line \\(x=1\\) force each sunny line to be used for a distinct point of that column). Consequently, after the non‑sunny lines are removed, all \\(k\\) sunny lines are already “forced’’ to cover the column \\(x=1\\); there is no remaining sunny line that could also cover any point that was eliminated by a non‑sunny line. This contradicts the presence of the \\(n-k\\ge 2\\) non‑sunny lines in the original configuration.  \n\n   Therefore no configuration exists for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Open cases.**  \n   The arguments above do not settle the possibilities \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\). A covering of the triangular set \\(S_n\\) by exactly \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to meet the lower bound of Lemma 1 with equality while still allowing the required number of non‑sunny lines (for \\(k=n-1\\)) or none (for \\(k=n\\)). I was unable to produce such coverings nor to prove that they cannot exist.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe values of \\(k\\) that are known to be attainable are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\); all values \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) are impossible. The status of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remains unresolved.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \