sider the second column \\(x=2\\), which contains \\(n-1\\) points.  \n   \u2013 At most \\(k\\) of them are covered by sunny lines.  \n   \u2013 Each of the remaining non\u2011sunny lines can meet this column in at most one point.  \n   Therefore we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-1\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)-k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality holds in (1); consequently **all** the \\(n-k-1\\) remaining non\u2011sunny lines are already used to cover distinct points of the second column, and none of them can be employed elsewhere.\n\n5. **Final contradiction for \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Because the non\u2011sunny lines are exhausted by the points of column\u202f2, every point with \\(x\\ge 3\\) must be covered by sunny lines only.  \n   The column \\(x=3\\) contains \\(n-2\\) points, but we have only \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which can meet this column in at most one point (a sunny line is never vertical). Hence we need  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2\\;\\le\\;k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (2) with the hypothesis \\(k\\ge 2\\) gives \\(k\\ge n-2\\).\n\n   Now look at the third column \\(x=3\\) again.  \n   When \\(k=n-2\\) the equality in (2) holds, so every sunny line must meet column\u202f3 in **exactly one** point, i.e. the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are forced to be the \\(n-2\\) distinct lines through the points \\((3,1),(3,2),\\dots ,(3,n-2)\\).\n\n   Each of those lines also passes through the point \\((1,\\,\\cdot)\\) of the first column (because a line through \\((3,b)\\) and \\((1,c)\\) has slope \\((b-c)/2\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(1\\le c\\le n\\)). Consequently every sunny line already contains a point of the first column, contradicting the fact that the first column is completely covered by the *single* vertical line \\(x=1\\) (the vertical line is the only non\u2011sunny line that can contain more than one point of that column).  \n\n   Hence a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) cannot exist.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible