------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of the vertical line\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\ge 2\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N\\) be the set of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nIf the vertical line \\(x=1\\) does **not** belong to \\(\\mathcal N\\), then each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point, and each sunny line meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point as well.  \nConsequently the \\(n\\) lines together can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\); but \\(|C_1|=n\\), so every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would have to be used solely for a distinct point of \\(C_1\\).  \nNo line would remain to cover any point outside the first column, contradicting the fact that \\(S_n\\) contains points with \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n\nHence the vertical line \\(x=1\\) **must** be one of the non\u2011sunny lines.  \nFrom now on we fix this line and denote the remaining non\u2011sunny lines by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N'=\\mathcal N\\setminus\\{x=1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal N'|=n-k-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustion of the second column\n\nConsider the second column  \n\n\\[\nC_2=\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\},\n\\qquad |C_2|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAt most \\(k\\) points of \\(C_2\\) can be covered by the sunny lines (each sunny line meets \\(C_2\\) in at most one point).  \nEvery line of \\(\\mathcal N'\\) meets \\(C_2\\) in at most one point (a horizontal line meets it in exactly one point, a diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\) also meets it in exactly one point).  \nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-k-1\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)-k .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nEquality holds in (4.1); consequently **every** line of \\(\\mathcal N'\\) is already used to cover a distinct point of \\(C_2\\).  \nNo line of \\(\\mathcal N'\\) is available for any other column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Columns three and higher\n\nFor any column \\(x=m\\) with \\(m\\ge 3\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nC_m=\\{(m,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-m\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1-m\\) points.  \nAll non\u2011sunny lines are exhausted (they are either the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the lines of \\(\\mathcal N'\\), which are already occupied by points of \\(C_2\\)).  \nHence every point of \\(C_m\\) must be covered by a sunny line.\n\nA sunny line is never vertical, so it meets each column in **at most one** point.  \nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of any column.  \nIn particular, for the third column we need  \n\n\\[\nn-2\\;\\le\\;k .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause we assumed \\(k\\ge 2\\), (5.1) forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Impossibility when \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nSuppose \\(k=n-2\\) (the smallest value compatible with (5.2)).  \nThen the equality in (5.1) holds, so each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines meets the third column in **exactly one** point; consequently the sunny lines are precisely the \\(n-2\\) distinct lines through the points  \n\n\\[\n(3,1),\\;(3,2),\\dots ,\\;(3,n-2).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nTake any two points from the list (6.1), say \\((3,b_1)\\) and \\((3,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\).  \nBoth lie on the same vertical line \\(x=3\\); a line through \\((3,b_1)\\) and a point of the first column \\((1,c)\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_1-c}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each line of (6.1) already contains a point of the first column.  \nConsequently the vertical line \\(x=1\\) would contain **more than one** point of the first column, contradicting the fact established in Section\u202f2 that the vertical line is the **only** non\u2011sunny line capable of containing more than one point of that column while all other non\u2011sunny lines are already used elsewhere.\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) cannot exist.  \nBecause any admissible \\(k\\ge 2\\) must satisfy \\(k\\ge n-2\\) (Section\u202f5) and the case \\(k=n-2\\) is impossible, there is **no** configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final answer\n\nThe only values of \\(k\\) for which a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) exists are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1 } .\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable by the explicit constructions given in Section\u202f1. \u220e"
